Check the input of the GGUF reader and keep the writer in step with it
Fold-into: upstream/gguf

The defects. tests/fuzz/core/run.sh test replays the reproducer of each one.

- The reader stores values of the file in enums before the range check, and reads a tensor type that a short file does not give.

  The reader converts an int32_t of the file to gguf_type and to ggml_type, and checks the range
  after the conversion. A value outside the range of the enum is undefined behavior at the load.
  The KV loop starts with gguf_type(-1), which is also outside the range. When the read of a
  tensor type fails (a short file), info.t.type holds no value, and the range check reads it.

  The change: The two enum reads check the int32_t before the conversion and refuse a value outside the range.
  The KV loop starts with GGUF_TYPE_COUNT. The tensor loop stops when the read of the type fails.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_gguf--gguf-enum-load, fuzz_model_load--gguf-enum-load, fuzz_gguf--gguf-reader-uninit-type.

- The reader accepts a key with a NUL byte.

  The reader accepts a key that holds a NUL byte. The key API takes C strings, thus gguf_find_key
  cannot find such a key, and gguf_set_kv makes an empty or a duplicate key from it. The next
  write of the context stops the process on GGML_ASSERT(!key.empty()).

  The change: The reader refuses a key that holds a NUL byte, as it refuses an empty key.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_gguf--gguf-key-nul.

- The reader accepts an alignment up to 2^31, which the writer pads one byte at a time.

  The reader accepts each power of 2 up to 2^31 as general.alignment. The writer pads the metadata
  one byte at a time, and gguf_get_meta_size writes the metadata to measure it. A file of 162
  bytes with an alignment of 2^30 thus makes the writer pad 1 GiB, one byte at a time.

  The change: The reader refuses an alignment above GGUF_MAX_ALIGNMENT (1 MiB).

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_gguf--gguf-huge-alignment.

- The guard of the element count multiplies the dimensions with an int64_t overflow.

  The guard of the element count starts with ggml_nelements(&info.t) > 0. That product of the
  four dimensions can overflow int64_t (undefined behavior) and wrap to 0 or below, and then the
  guard is off. A tensor of 0 bytes then gets no data pointer, and the writer stops on
  GGML_ASSERT(info.t.data).

  The change: The guard reads the dimensions when their reads succeed: a dimension of 0 skips the check,
  and each other tensor gets it.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_gguf--gguf-nelements-overflow.

- The reader allocates the declared data size before it checks the length of the input.

  With no_alloc = false, the reader allocates the ggml context for the declared data size before
  it reads the data. A file of 96 bytes that declares a large tensor asks for that size.

  The change: The reader refuses the file when the declared data size is larger than the input after the header.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_gguf--gguf-early-alloc.

- A write of general.alignment does not change the alignment of the context and the tensor offsets.

  gguf_set_val_u32 and gguf_set_kv write the key general.alignment, but ctx->alignment and the
  offsets of the tensors keep the old value. The writer then pads the data with one alignment and
  the file says a different one, and the reader refuses the file that the writer wrote.

  The change: A write of general.alignment sets ctx->alignment and computes the tensor offsets again. A remove
  of the key goes back to the default alignment.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_gguf--gguf-alignment.

- The writer drops a dimension of 0 of a tensor.

  The writer writes ggml_n_dims(t) dimensions. ggml_n_dims counts a dimension of 0 as 1, thus a
  tensor with ne[1] == 0 is written as 1-D and comes back with ne[1] == 1.

  The change: The writer writes each dimension up to the last one that is not 1.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_gguf--gguf-zero-dim.

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

